// A REPL over the board's USB serial port.

package {
  name    = "repl"
  version = "0.1.0"
}

dependencies {
  pico2 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/pico2", version = "0.0.7" }
}

// **`heap = true`, and it is the one thing separating this program from the blink example.**
//
// Two things want it. The line is grown as it is typed — `sysl.term.edit` keeps it in a `Buf`, which
// is what lets a character be inserted in the middle of one already typed. And picking the line apart
// wants more: `split` answers a `[]string`, an allocation for the slice and one per word, and
// `from_utf8` makes a `string`. A microcontroller has no allocator of its own; this one is newlib's,
// which the SDK links, so the capability is honest rather than free.
//
// A genuinely allocation-free REPL is reachable — read into a fixed buffer, compare the input as
// bytes and parse the number by hand — and is what a target with no heap at all would need.
//
// **The word is `heap` here and `@no_alloc` in a source file, and the two are different on purpose.**
// This file states whether a facility *exists*, which is a noun beside `os` and `threads`; a module
// giving one up is promising something about its own conduct, which is a verb. Writing `alloc` here
// is refused, and the refusal says so.
requires {
  heap = true
}
